  "stage": "study-review",
  "summary": "Cold review of the canonical expansive study for the Eighteenth Sunday after Pentecost (1962): main.tex, 34 physical pages (author-study-0003 PDF 2e9a130e...; every sealed REVIEW_INPUTS hash matched the tree). No blocking defect remains. research/interpretations.md now records the study's readings on every point STU-020 named: Hilary, In Matth. VIII.8, as a witness of the third reading; the withdrawal of the three statements in 4.3 and the Chrysostom-Hilary difference as the one disagreement that runs between two readings; 1 Cor 1:8 and perficias as anchors of the third reading's anagogical sense only; and Chrysostom's 'not only the beginnings ... but the end also' on 1 Cor 1:7. Both repairs made at this iteration are accurate. STU-021: the Ambrose passage (Exp. in Lc. V.15) was checked in ambrose-luke5-raw.txt, SHA-256 fde2303a...6330ac52 recomputed; 'Spectant surgentem increduli ... quia non diligebant, calumniabantur' is quoted correctly in the second reading and in the comparison. STU-022: the Sextuplex placings match scope 2.3 (R B K S for the Gradual, Offertory and Communion; R B K for the Introit). Spot checks also hold for Hilary VIII.7-8 (PL 9 layer db389fea), for Aquinas, Super Matth. IX (Venice 1745 layer: omnia in Deum retulerunt, quia portabatur praecepit ut portaret, operationis miraculorum), for Chrysostom, Hom. in Mt. 29, and for Augustine, De cons. evang. II.25.57-58 (CCEL NPNF). The study's transmission claims agree with scope 2.1-2.3 and 2.7. Limits: page images were not reopened. Components outside REVIEW_SCOPE (00, 05, 20, 40, 80, 90) were read for coherence only. Two advisories are raised: the record's residual drift from the study at 2.4 Literal and 1.2, and two sentences that appear nearly verbatim in both the element section and the second reading."
}
